  "summary": "Vectorman is BlueSky Software's action platform game, first released for Genesis in North America on 24 October 1995.",
  "body": [
    "Vectorman is BlueSky Software's action platform game, first released for Genesis in North America on 24 October 1995. Its hero is a robot working on an Earth abandoned by humanity and left to machines for cleaning. When those machines fall under hostile control, Vectorman travels through the planet's industrial and natural environments to stop them. Running, jumping and shooting form the basis of the journey, with a character assembled from rounded shapes that gives the animation an unusually flexible appearance for a late Genesis release.",
    "Vectorman fires in eight directions, allowing him to attack enemies above or below while moving through the stage. Routes can branch across larger areas, with a time limit encouraging the player to keep searching and advancing. Power-ups change his weapons, increase their damage or transform his body into a machine with another way to move. A helicopter can take to the air, while a vehicle form offers speed. Finding an item can therefore alter the immediate route as well as provide a stronger answer to the next enemy.",
    "The hero's body and other objects use pre-rendered imagery, giving spheres, machinery and lighting a conspicuous presence on the older hardware. That presentation helped Sega show what the Genesis could still deliver as newer consoles arrived. Jon Holland's electronic soundtrack accompanies stages built around movement, target placement and changing forms. Vectorman's unusual appearance supports those mechanics: a robot made of separate rounded components can rearrange itself convincingly, making its transformations feel like extensions of the same character rather than unrelated vehicles appearing for a single scene."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "A robot left on Earth",
    "Changing shape and direction",
    "A late sixteen-bit showcase"
  ],
